        {
          "id": "bitwise-operators",
          "title": "Bitwise Operators (AND/OR/XOR/SHIFT)",
          "status": "sample",
          "content": {
            "introduction": [
              "Since the 2025 pattern overhaul, the pseudo code round is **calculation-heavy on bitwise operators**. You must trace bit-by-bit — $\\text{AND}$, $\\text{OR}$, $\\text{XOR}$, left shift $\\ll$ and right shift $\\gg$ — without a compiler.",
              "The conversion chain, worked out longhand: $$10110_2 = 1 \\cdot 16 + 0 \\cdot 8 + 1 \\cdot 4 + 1 \\cdot 2 + 0 \\cdot 1 = 22_{10}$$ and it is this single chain — left as $d$, $t$, $h$, $o$ on the ladder — that every bitwise problem reduces to.",
              "The conversion chain, worked out longhand: 39061510110_2 = 1 cdot 16 + 0 cdot 8 + 1 cdot 4 + 1 cdot 2 + 0 cdot 1 = 22_{10}390615 and it is this single chain — left as $, $, $, $ on the ladder — that every bitwise problem reduces to."
            ],
            "sections": [
              {
                "id": "binary-basics",
                "title": "Binary ↔ Decimal in 3 seconds",
                "blocks": [
                  {
                    "type": "formula",
                    "title": "Place-value expansion",
                    "latex": "d = \\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} b_i \\cdot 2^i",
                    "text": "Each bit $b_i$ (from the right, starting at $i=0$) contributes $2^i$ when set.",
                    "whenToUse": "Convert any binary number to decimal while tracing pseudocode.",
                    "explanation": [
                      "Read bits right-to-left: positions are $2^0, 2^1, 2^2, \\dots$",
                      "Only add powers whose bit is $1$."
                    ],
                    "example": {
                      "prompt": "Convert $10110_2$ to decimal.",
                      "steps": [
                        {
                          "text": "Write positions under the bits: $16\\;\\;8\\;\\;4\\;\\;2\\;\\;1$",
                          "reason": "Powers of two from the left."
                        },
                        {
                          "text": "Keep only positions with bit $1$: $16 + 4 + 2$",
                          "reason": "Bits at index 4, 2 and 1 are set."
                        },
                        {
                          "text": "Add: $16 + 4 + 2 = 22$"
                        }
                      ],
                      "answer": {
                        "latex": "10110_2 = 22_{10}"
                      }
                    },
                    "memoryTip": "Memorize the power ladder: 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128 — and reverse it for decimal → binary.",
                    "commonMistake": "Counting positions from 1 instead of 0 — $2^0 = 1$, not $2^1$."
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "table",
                    "headers": [
                      "Power",
                      "2^4",
                      "2^3",
                      "2^2",
                      "2^1",
                      "2^0"
                    ],
                    "rows": [
                      [
                        "Value",
                        "16",
                        "8",
                        "4",
                        "2",
                        "1"
                      ],
                      [
                        "Bit 10110",
                        "1",
                        "0",
                        "1",
                        "1",
                        "0"
                      ],
                      [
                        "Contribution",
                        "16",
                        "0",
                        "4",
                        "2",
                        "0"
                      ]
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "derivation",
                    "title": "The same conversion, textbook style",
                    "lines": [
                      {
                        "latex": "10110_2 = 1 \\cdot 2^4 + 0 \\cdot 2^3 + 1 \\cdot 2^2 + 1 \\cdot 2^1 + 0 \\cdot 2^0",
                        "note": "expand every bit by its place value"
                      },
                      {
                        "latex": "= 16 + 4 + 2",
                        "note": "drop the zero terms"
                      },
                      {
                        "latex": "= 22_{10}",
                        "note": "add left to right"
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "callout",
                    "kind": "tip",
                    "title": "Exam shortcut",
                    "content": [
                      "If the MSB (leftmost bit) is set, the number is at least half of the range. For an 8-bit view, $1000\\,0000_2 = 128$ exactly — anchor yourself to that.",
                      "Write the power ladder above every trace — 10 seconds saved per question."
                    ]
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "operator-rules",
                "title": "The four operators, exactly as Accenture tests them",
                "blocks": [
                  {
                    "type": "table",
                    "headers": [
                      "Operator",
                      "Pseudocode form",
                      "Rule",
                      "Example (a=12, b=5)"
                    ],
                    "rows": [
                      [
                        "AND",
                        "$a \\text{ AND } b$",
                        "Bit is 1 only when both bits are 1",
                        "$1100 \\& 0101 = 0100 = 4$"
                      ],
                      [
                        "OR",
                        "$a \\text{ OR } b$",
                        "Bit is 1 when at least one bit is 1",
                        "$1100 \\mid 0101 = 1101 = 13$"
